public static String escapeJava(final String string) {
    int strLen = string.length();
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(strLen);
    for (int i = 0; i < strLen; i++) {
        char c = string.charAt(i);
        switch (c) {
            case ' ': sb.append("\\b"); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\t"); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\n"); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\f"); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\r"); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\\""); break;
            case ' ': sb.append("\\\\"); break;
            default:
                if ((c < 32) || (c > 127)) {
                    String hex = Integer.toHexString(c);
                    sb.append("\\u");
                    for (int k = hex.length(); k < 4; k++) {
                        sb.append(' ');
                    }
                    sb.append(hex);
                } else {
                    sb.append(c);
                }
        }
    }
    return sb.toString();
}


**Summary:**
The `escapeJava` method takes a string as input and returns a new string with special characters escaped. It iterates through each character of the input string. If the character is a space, it appends `\\b`, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\f`, or `\\r` based on the type of space. If the character is a double quote (`"`), it appends `\\\"`. If the character is a backslash (`\`), it appends `\\\\`. For any other character that is not a printable ASCII character (ASCII values less than 32 or greater than 127), it appends the character in hexadecimal format prefixed with `\\u` and padded with spaces to ensure a 4-digit hexadecimal value. If the character is a printable ASCII character, it appends the character as is.

