@Override
public void close() {
    if (isClosed()) return;
    checkOpenness();
    if (ownerPool != null && ownerPool.getConnectionsInCurrentThread(getURL(), userName) > 1) {
        ownerPool.release(this);
        return;
    }
    try {
        commit(true);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        OLogManager.instance().error(this, "Error on releasing database '%s' in pool", e, getName());
    }
    try {
        callOnCloseListeners();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        OLogManager.instance().error(this, "Error on releasing database '%s' in pool", e, getName());
    }
    getLocalCache().clear();
    if (ownerPool != null) {
        final ODatabaseDocumentPool localCopy = ownerPool;
        ownerPool = null;
        localCopy.release(this);
    }
    ODatabaseRecordThreadLocal.instance().remove();
}


**Summary:**
The `close` method is designed to properly close a database connection. It first checks if the connection is already closed and returns if true. It then ensures the connection is open. If the connection is part of a pool and there are other connections in the pool for the same database and user, it releases the connection back to the pool and returns. If not, it attempts to commit the transaction and handles any exceptions that occur during the commit. It also calls any close listeners and handles exceptions for them. After that, it clears the local cache and removes the connection from the thread local storage.

