public static String num(Number count) {
    if (count == null) return "";
    if (count instanceof Double || count instanceof BigDecimal) {
        String s = count.toString();
        if (s.charAt(1) == ' ' && s.length() > 7) {
            s = s.substring(0, 5);
        }
        return s;
    } else if (count instanceof Integer || count instanceof Long || count instanceof Short || count instanceof BigInteger) {
        String s = count.toString();
        s = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
        CharBuf buf = CharBuf.create(s.length());
        int index = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            index++;
            buf.add(c);
            if (index % 3 == 0) {
                buf.add(' ');
            }
        }
        if (buf.lastChar() == ' ') {
            buf.removeLastChar();
        }
        s = buf.toString();
        s = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
        return s;
    }
    return count.toString();
}


**Summary:**
The `num` method takes a `Number` object as input and returns a formatted string. If the input is `null`, it returns an empty string. If the input is a `Double` or `BigDecimal`, it checks if the string representation of the number has a space at the second position and a length greater than 7. If so, it truncates the string to the first 5 characters. If the input is an `Integer`, `Long`, `Short`, or `BigInteger`, it reverses the string, inserts spaces every three characters, and then reverses it back. If the input is of any other type, it simply returns the string representation of the number.

